From: Runeon Contributors <dev@runeon.com>
Subject: [PATCH] msvcrt: Keep refusing code page 54936 for locales and _setmbcp().

setlocale() and _setmbcp() accept any code page IsValidCodePage() and
GetCPInfo() accept, and then build the CRT's character tables assuming at
most two bytes per character. Code page 54936 (GB18030) was rejected
there only because kernelbase did not know it; now that it does, the CRT
would build a locale whose lead-byte, case and multibyte tables are wrong
for four-byte sequences.

Microsoft documents that the CRT supports no multibyte code page beyond
two bytes except UTF-8, so refuse 54936 explicitly in both places. Programs
that fell back to another locale when this failed keep doing so.

--- a/dlls/msvcrt/locale.c
+++ b/dlls/msvcrt/locale.c
@@ -431,6 +431,11 @@
     }
     if (!IsValidCodePage(locale_cp))
         return FALSE;
+    /* The CRT tables only describe code pages of up to two bytes per
+     * character, and UTF-8 separately; GB18030 is valid but has four-byte
+     * sequences, so it is refused as it was before kernelbase knew it. */
+    if (locale_cp == 54936)
+        return FALSE;
 
     if (!locale_cp)
         return FALSE;
--- a/dlls/msvcrt/mbcs.c
+++ b/dlls/msvcrt/mbcs.c
@@ -275,7 +275,8 @@
     mbcinfo->mblcid = 0;
   }
 
-  if (!GetCPInfo(newcp, &cpi))
+  /* see locale_to_sname(): GB18030 does not fit the two-byte tables */
+  if (newcp == 54936 || !GetCPInfo(newcp, &cpi))
   {
     WARN("Codepage %d not found\n", newcp);
     free(mbcinfo);
